# x-residual-moves-b core wiring

## 1. Module line
native/core/crates/mixdog-core-domains/src/domains/mod.rs: beside `pub mod desktop_residual;` add

    pub mod desktop_residual_b;

(desktop_residual_b.rs declares its own tests, desktop_residual_b_tests.rs.)

## 2. Cargo: one dependency of mixdog-core-domains
The thumbnail decodes and re-encodes JPEG with the workspace's `image` (features = ["jpeg"], already in [workspace.dependencies] and Cargo.lock;
mixdog-tools uses it for the set-of-marks encoder). In crates/mixdog-core-domains/Cargo.toml [dependencies], beside `futures`:

    image = { workspace = true }

and in Cargo.lock add `"image",` to the dependency list of the `mixdog-core-domains` package (alphabetical place). The tests use it too
(`image::codecs::jpeg::JpegEncoder`, `image::load_from_memory`). `mixdog-workspace`, `mixdog-core-config`, `url`, `sha2`, `base64`, `futures`, `tracing`
and `async-trait` are already dependencies of the crate. If the workspace-hack crate is kept in step by hand, `image` with feature "jpeg" is in it already
through mixdog-tools.

## 3. A visibility edit in a file the task does not own
native/core/crates/mixdog-workspace/src/preview.rs: `fn file_preview_type(path: &Path) -> Option<(&'static str, &'static str)>` becomes `pub fn` (it is the one
copy of `FILE_PREVIEW_TYPES` of shared/file-preview.ts; `desktop/localLink/open` calls `mixdog_workspace::preview::file_preview_type` for the
editor-versus-OS decision of `editorFileOpener`). The crate does not compile until this is applied. `preview` is already `pub mod preview;` in lib.rs.

## 4. Registration (compose_flip.rs, after the DesktopResidualService registration)
The service reads attachments through the same AttachmentHost as DesktopResidualService; `b.data_dir` holds mixdog-config.json and the native tool caches:

    builder.register(Arc::new(mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::DesktopResidualBService::new(
        Arc::new(mixdog_core_session::session::attachments::DesktopAttachmentHost::new(directory.clone(), blobs.clone(), &b.tokens)),
        &b.tokens,
        b.data_dir.clone(),
    )))?; // `desktop/localLink/open`, `desktop/attachment/write|thumbnail`, `desktop/launchVersion/*`, `desktop/modelBootstrap/read`, `desktop/nativeToolCache/gc`, `desktop/userFile/write`, `desktop/computer/inputAccess/request`

## 5. SERVICE_PENDING
Nothing to remove: the nine method names are new in pending-rows/x-residual-moves-b.rows and the service that answers them is registered by section 4.
Apply the rows and this registration together (a protocol method without a registered service, or a service method not in the table, fails the compose check).

## 6. Library parts the launcher and the relay leg take (no method)
- `ImportProgress` (conversation-import-progress.ts): `ImportProgress::new(Arc<dyn ImportJobSource>, Duration::from_millis(CONVERSATION_IMPORT_POLL_MS))`. The
  node-capability call site (mixdog-app-server node_calls.rs: `import/startConversationImport`, `import/cancelConversationImport`, `import/getConversationImportJobs`)
  calls `progress.observe(capability, &result)` after each of those calls; its ImportJobSource fetches the job list with the same capability
  and publishes `conversation-import-progress` (CONVERSATION_IMPORT_PROGRESS_EVENT) on whatever carries a desktop event today.
- `NetworkWatcher::start(Duration::from_millis(NETWORK_POLL_MS), Arc::new(network_snapshot), on_change)` for the relay leg (mixdog-remote): `on_change` redials
  at once. The OS interface list of network-watch.ts is replaced by the routing table's answer for each address family (a connected UDP socket sends nothing):
  online is "a route exists", the signature is the local address of the route; a Wi-Fi switch, a VPN toggle or a dock changes it.
- `read_login_shell_environment("linux" | "darwin", None, LOGIN_SHELL_READ_BUDGET)`, `login_shell_overrides(inherited, shell)` and `with_homebrew_path(path, exists)`
  (login-shell-environment.ts): they run before the daemon is spawned, so the launcher calls them and applies the overrides to the daemon's environment. The launcher
  (mixdog-spawn or the daemon start) must depend on mixdog-core-domains for that, or the three functions move to a crate it already depends on: a dependency edge
  and a call site the lead decides. The functions are synchronous and spawn a shell, so they must not run on a core executor thread.

## 7. Client side (x-residual-moves-b-client)
The desktop callers (ipc-file-dialogs.ts, ipc-projects.ts, ipc-sessions.ts, index.ts, the launch-record and model-bootstrap call sites) call the nine methods; the OS hand-off
(`shell.openPath(path)` for a `{result: "file" | "folder", path}` answer, and the "Unable to open file: <failure>" error when it returns one) stays with Electron.
Deleting the TypeScript files and their tests is the client task's, after its parity check. The rows already in Rust (agent-memory, approval-grant-change,
child-environment, feedback-client, git-contract, ipc-control-chars, mixy-first, session-sidebar-actions) are named in the module header of desktop_residual_b.rs
with where they live.
